.Dd October 12, 2026
.Dt RDOMAIN 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rdomain
.Nd routing domains
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "ifconfig interface rdomain N"
.Cd "route -T N exec command"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A routing domain is an isolated set of routing tables and interfaces.
An interface belongs to exactly one routing domain, a routing domain can
contain several routing tables, and the same network address can be used
in several routing domains at once.
.Pp
On 5BSD a routing table is a FIB
.Pq Xr setfib 2 .
Routing domain
.Ar N
is FIB
.Ar N
made a domain of its own; the other FIBs are routing tables of the
domain they belong to, which is domain 0 unless stated otherwise.
FIBs created with
.Va net.fibs
or by
.Nm route Fl T Ar N Cm add
belong to routing domain 0, so the
.Fx
FIB behaviour is unchanged
until a routing domain is created.
Routing domain numbers range from 0 to 255.
.Pp
A routing domain is created by moving an interface into it with
.Nm ifconfig Ar interface Cm rdomain Ar N .
The routing table must not hold routes yet.
Creating routing domain
.Ar N
also creates its loopback interface
.Li lo Ns Ar N ,
which stays in the domain and cannot be destroyed.
Moving an interface to another routing domain removes its addresses and
the routes through it, since they belong to the old domain.
.Pp
Inside a routing domain:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Packets received on an interface use the domain's routing table
.Pq the interface's FIB .
.It
An address is local only in the routing domain of its interface:
packets for an address of another domain are not delivered locally, and
sockets can only bind to addresses of their own domain.
.It
Sockets only receive packets of their own domain.
The routing table of a process or socket is selected with
.Xr setfib 2 ,
.Xr setfib 1 ,
.Dv SO_SETFIB
.Pq also known as Dv SO_RTABLE ,
.Nm route Fl T Ar N Cm exec
or
.Nm ping Fl V Ar N .
Entering a routing table of another routing domain requires privilege;
changing tables inside a domain does not.
.It
Local traffic is looped through the domain's loopback interface
.Li lo Ns Ar N .
.El
.Pp
.Xr pf 4
evaluates rules per routing domain: the
.Cm on rdomain Ar N
rule option matches packets of a domain, the
.Cm rtable Ar N
option moves packets to another routing table, and state keys include the
routing domain.
.Pp
The routing domain of every routing table is reported by the
.Dv NET_RT_TABLE
.Xr sysctl 3
.Pq Va net.route.0.0.1024.N ,
which returns a
.Vt struct rt_tableinfo .
.Sh EXAMPLES
Put
.Li vether0
into routing domain 3 and run a shell there:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# ifconfig vether0 rdomain 3
# ifconfig vether0 192.0.2.1/24 up
# route -T 3 exec sh
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr setfib 1 ,
.Xr setfib 2 ,
.Xr lo 4 ,
.Xr pf 4 ,
.Xr vether 4 ,
.Xr pf.conf 5 ,
.Xr ifconfig 8 ,
.Xr ping 8 ,
.Xr route 8
.Sh HISTORY
Routing domains appeared in
.Ox 4.9 .
They were added to 5BSD, on top of
.Fx
FIBs, in 2026.
